ct  and then we carry the carried on doing  further experiments to try to understand  the mechanism behind this and what we  show is that rhinovirus triggers a  transient  innate immune response which is mediated  by the interf the by interferon  and that blocks the replication of a  starscope too  then to try to understand the relevance  of this because  our work was experimental uh we  collaborated with imperial college  london and  they use mathematical models to try to  determine what the epidemiological  impact of these  interactions could be and what the  mathematical models show is that  in when you have high prevalence of  rhinovirus  common codes that would cause a decrease  in the number of new covet 19 cases  so so effectively a rhinovirus is a  relatively selfish virus and  and it wants you to itself it says  there's no room for anything else  how in real life in the real world  outside  of laboratory could this be used to try  and reduce case numbers  well the the best tool we have to reduce  case numbers is without any doubt  the vaccines okay so this will be the  main  barrier for a starscope to  to spread in the population then on top  of that  what we think as an additional barrier  these virus will have to compete  with other respiratory viruses and we  have quite a few respiratory viruses  that circulate commonly  in the population influenza a b  respiratory incision virus para  influenza viruses etc  because of the measures that we've been  taking though professor over  the course of the last year with social  distancing with increased  hand hygiene for example have we seen  rates of those common colds of influenza  falling  well uh yes the what we have seen  is that the all the intervention  measures that have been put in place  have uh reduce significantly the  transmission of  most respiratory viruses yes  a common cold we we all accept it it's  part of  life um it's also relatively short-lived  isn't it  does that then mean it only gives  protection for a short period of time  that's correct  so this this innate immune response is  is a response that is triggered by the  cells  when when they get infected or when they  sense a vital viral infection  and this is a short-lived response the  the effect that we  think might happen at the population  level is based on the high level the  high transmissibility of  of the the common cold virus okay  and fascinating talking to you who would  have thought we'd be embracing the  common  cole but professor pablo mercier thank  you very much  indeed thank you
 

